WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
Question here in the chat.

00:03.000 --> 00:10.000
How do life cycle hooks like before destroy or destroy work inside the Composition API?

00:10.000 --> 00:12.000
Yeah, this is a great question.

00:12.000 --> 00:17.000
So if we look inside of our life cycle hooks, oh, do I have it all up already?

00:17.000 --> 00:21.000
The way to think about it is that Composition API doesn't actually,

00:21.000 --> 00:24.000
as much as we're talking about it as like a separate way of architecting your apps,

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
it doesn't break away from how the component works.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
So the question here regarding like before unmount,

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
so basically destroy instead of destroy, think of it as unmounted.

00:34.000 --> 00:36.000
All that stuff still happens at the end.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
The only thing that's different about the Composition API is that it just happens at the very beginning.

00:40.000 --> 00:43.000
It's still all that other stuff you expect when it comes to destroying stuff

00:43.000 --> 00:45.000
that still factors into Composition API.

00:45.000 --> 00:49.000
In fact, Composition API will continue to hit all these other life cycle hooks.

00:49.000 --> 00:53.000
So just to answer this question a little bit more fully,

00:53.000 --> 00:57.000
let me go to the Pokedex here that has the script set up.

00:57.000 --> 01:01.000
So if in the event, you're saying, oh, well, this component, let's say,

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
let me put this side by side so we can actually see this together.

01:04.000 --> 01:07.000
And then let me reduce that so it's easier to read.

01:07.000 --> 01:10.000
So if you're trying to say that like, OK, I have this thing where like,

01:10.000 --> 01:14.000
when the right before the component unmounts, I want it to do something.

01:14.000 --> 01:17.000
Sometimes that's a thing before it's destroyed.

01:17.000 --> 01:20.000
The way you would do that is you need a helper method.

01:20.000 --> 01:23.000
And so we have, just as you would expect it to be called,

01:23.000 --> 01:25.000
it's not called before unmount.

01:25.000 --> 01:29.000
Because what we realize with conventions is that this is really a life cycle event.

01:29.000 --> 01:33.000
So really, all of our stuff are prefixed with on.

01:33.000 --> 01:35.000
Like you would expect for other events, on click, on key up.

01:35.000 --> 01:38.000
Well, since this is a helper method for an event for life cycle hooks,

01:38.000 --> 01:41.000
on before mount is what we would import.

01:41.000 --> 01:43.000
And then we'll, sorry, on before unmount,

01:43.000 --> 01:46.000
because that's particularly what we were talking about.

01:46.000 --> 01:49.000
So we would say on before unmount.

01:49.000 --> 01:51.000
There we go.

01:51.000 --> 01:56.000
And then I usually like to keep my life cycle hooks towards the bottom, usually.

01:56.000 --> 01:58.000
But expect you a composition API.

01:58.000 --> 02:00.000
That goes out the window depending on how complex the code is.

02:00.000 --> 02:03.000
So it'd be very clear that it's not at all a best practice,

02:03.000 --> 02:07.000
just kind of what I'm used to doing from the options API.

02:07.000 --> 02:10.000
So what we would do is we would call on before unmount.

02:10.000 --> 02:15.000
And then it's a function that takes a callback function of what we want to do.

02:15.000 --> 02:18.000
So in this particular case, you might be like, do this thing.

02:19.000 --> 02:21.000
And then that's it.

02:21.000 --> 02:23.000
You've done, essentially, what you would do on an options API,

02:23.000 --> 02:25.000
you just define it at the setup level.

02:25.000 --> 02:28.000
So the key thing, again, to remember is that composition API

02:28.000 --> 02:30.000
does not take you out of the flow.

02:30.000 --> 02:32.000
You're absolutely bought into the whole thing.

02:32.000 --> 02:36.000
It's just now you can literally set everything up right here at this one step,

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
and then let it run from there.

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
Whereas as we saw with options API,

02:41.000 --> 02:44.000
there is kind of a little bit more intricacy regarding do your setup,

02:44.000 --> 02:46.000
then pass that into your options,

02:46.000 --> 02:48.000
and then options can do stuff from there.

02:48.000 --> 02:50.000
So it's a style thing.

02:50.000 --> 02:52.000
You'll figure out what works best for you.

02:52.000 --> 02:56.000
But what I've done in this workshop is try to present you with the different styles

02:56.000 --> 03:00.000
so that you can get a feel for it and figure out what you want for your apps.

03:00.000 --> 03:03.000
Hopefully that helps answer that question.

